import { BytesReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesReaderCopy = BytesReaderCopy {
  summary: "Use std/bytes/hex to put a byte sequence into a log or text field as hexadecimal. When reading it back, receive an invalid digit or length separately from a successful Bytes value.",
  taskTitle: "Make a byte sequence readable as hexadecimal",
  purpose: "Suppose you want to record file or network data as text that shows its original byte values. One byte holds a value from 0 through 255. Hexadecimal uses two digits per byte, so even zero is recorded as 00 with its leading zero intact.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript uses Uint8Array.from to create bytes from the fixed array of numbers, then toHex() to produce lowercase hexadecimal. Uint8Array is a byte array whose elements can be changed. Every number in this example is already in range, so no additional validation is needed.",
  benefit: "TypeScript’s toHex() is already concise. Seseragi passes an immutable Bytes value to hex.encode. fromInts checks the numbers, while encode gives the validated byte sequence its text representation.",
  setup: "bytes aliases std/bytes and hex aliases std/bytes/hex. import * as assigns those aliases; a name such as hex.encode selects an operation from that module. This example displays five bytes and then an empty byte sequence.",
  after: [
    "fn render values: Array<Int> -> String defines a function that takes an integer array named values and returns a string. fn starts the definition, the type after : describes the argument, and the type after -> describes the result. Int means integer, Array<Int> is an array of integers, and String is text.",
    "bytes.fromInts values calls a function by placing its argument after a space. fromInts checks the range and returns either Right content for success or Left error for failure. Either is the type for these two possible results. match selects the branch for the result that arrived.",
    "The Right content branch names the successful Bytes value content and returns hex.encode content. The Left error branch uses show error to turn the reason into display text. The expression after each branch’s -> becomes that branch’s result and the result of render.",
    "The values 0, 15, 16, 171, and 255 become 00, 0f, 10, ab, and ff, producing 000f10abff. The empty array produces an empty string, so the next line is blank. Parentheses in println (render values) call render first and pass its result to printing.",
  ],
  details: [
    "encode represents each byte with lowercase ASCII digits 0–9 and a–f. It adds no 0x prefix, separators, or whitespace. It creates a new string with twice as many digits as the number of input bytes.",
    "The reverse operation, decode, accepts uppercase and lowercase hexadecimal digits. Reading 00FF and writing it again with encode gives 00ff. Canonical here means the one spelling the encoder chooses for those bytes.",
    "decode first checks that the input’s UTF-8 byte length is even, then checks the digits. Failure is a HexDecodeError inside Left. OddHexLength carries the input length; InvalidHexDigit carries an offset that counts byte positions from zero.",
    "decode rejects 0x00 and 00 00. If a sender uses such a format, check its specification before handling it separately. Decoding hexadecimal also does not prove that the bytes are text. Use std/text’s decodeUtf8 separately before treating them as a string.",
    "Hexadecimal does not encrypt or compress data. These conversions work in browsers and processes without an input/output service. Only println in the example performs output.",
  ],
  nodeNote: "The inspected Node 24 runtime has no toHex()/fromHex(). With Node/Bun, import { Buffer } from \"node:buffer\" and write Buffer.from(content).toString(\"hex\"). Buffer is a Uint8Array subclass with conversion methods. Buffer.from(input, \"hex\") may truncate at invalid input, so check for even length and ASCII hexadecimal digits before reading.",
}
